$DIR/main.p:24:47: cannot convert n (value of type *int32) to *MyFd: the pointer gives write access to an owner of type sys.Fd outside package sys
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. A pointer conversion from *int32 to
    *MyFd lets code read and write the same object as either type. A write
    through it could make a sys.Fd from a plain value, or replace one, so the
    conversion is allowed only where int32 converts to MyFd and MyFd converts to
    int32. Only the package that declares sys.Fd may do that, where its
    invariants are kept.
    Use the functions and methods of the package that declares sys.Fd.
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E3212" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:26:47: cannot convert s (value of union type FakeSlot) to owner type MySlot: it holds a new sys.Slot, which only package sys can make
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. MySlot is defined from the owner type
    sys.Slot, so a new MySlot holds a new sys.Slot. Converting a FakeSlot into a
    MySlot is allowed only where converting it into a sys.Slot is allowed too,
    and only the package that declares sys.Slot may make one, where its
    invariants are kept.
    Convert a value that is already a sys.Slot, made by the package that
    declares it:
        MySlot(sys.SlotValue)
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E3212" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:28:52: cannot convert fd (value of int32 type sys.Fd) to owner type sys.Inner outside package sys
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. Converting the owner sys.Fd into a
    sys.Inner moves it, but the result is a sys.Inner that nobody in its package
    made. sys.Fd is made by the rules of another package, perhaps from any
    number, so the conversion could forge a sys.Inner. Only the package that
    declares sys.Inner may do that, where its invariants are kept, or the
    package of a type defined from sys.Inner.
    Use a constructor from the package that declares sys.Inner.
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E3212" for a longer explanation)

